### Current behavior?

Hello,

I run inference with TFLite on the CPU of a Raspberry Pi5.
I noticed a fast difference in behavior when using multiple threads in C++ vs Python.

When I set :
interpreter = tf.lite.Interpreter(model_path=args.model, num_threads=4) `
  all 4 threads use the CPU to 80%+. (manually checked in htop)

But when I use the following C++ Code, only 1 thread is at 65%, while all other 3 do not go over 5%. As such, the c++ model is alot slower than Python.

C++
```

model = tflite::FlatBufferModel::BuildFromFile(model_path);
if (!model) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Failed to load model");
}

tflite::ops::builtin::BuiltinOpResolver resolver;
tflite::InterpreterBuilder(*model, resolver)(&_interpreter);

_interpreter->SetNumThreads(4);

if (!_interpreter) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Failed to build interpreter");
}

// Create XNNPACK delegate
TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptions options = TfLiteXNNPackDelegateOptionsDefault();
options.num_threads = 4;

TfLiteDelegate* xnnpack_delegate = TfLiteXNNPackDelegateCreate(&options);
if (!xnnpack_delegate) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Failed to create XNNPACK delegate");
}

if (_interpreter->ModifyGraphWithDelegate(xnnpack_delegate) != kTfLiteOk) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Failed to apply XNNPACK delegate");
}


if (_interpreter->AllocateTensors() != kTfLiteOk) {
    throw std::runtime_error("AllocateTensors failed");
}
```



Am I missing something?

